1
00:00:24,061 --> 00:00:33,129
[SPEAKER_03]: Hello, everyone, and welcome to your show, Method Happenings, a show far and about the city of Method, and I am John Petrella.

2
00:00:33,649 --> 00:00:48,322
[SPEAKER_03]: And the purpose of our program is to give Method citizens facts and information to help you make informed choices to discover city services or businesses that you may not have been aware of.

3
00:00:49,023 --> 00:00:50,504
[SPEAKER_03]: And we got a special guest today.

4
00:00:52,268 --> 00:01:01,400
[SPEAKER_03]: Our guest is Matt McLaughlin, who is a candidate for the second Middlesex District State Senator.

5
00:01:01,961 --> 00:01:03,864
[SPEAKER_03]: Matt, thank you for joining us.

6
00:01:03,904 --> 00:01:04,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you for having me, John.

7
00:01:05,125 --> 00:01:06,427
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a pleasure to have you here.

8
00:01:07,989 --> 00:01:10,733
[SPEAKER_03]: So we're just going to get right into this, Matt, okay?

9
00:01:10,773 --> 00:01:12,655
[SPEAKER_03]: We're just going to go right into questions.

10
00:01:15,038 --> 00:01:19,104
[SPEAKER_03]: So if you can, just tell us a little bit about yourself.

11
00:01:19,905 --> 00:01:22,228
[SPEAKER_03]: Who is Matt McLaughlin?

12
00:01:22,268 --> 00:01:24,531
[SPEAKER_03]: People want to know, who is Matt McLaughlin?

13
00:01:24,931 --> 00:01:28,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Medford, I am Matt McLaughlin and I'm your cousin from Somerville.

14
00:01:28,696 --> 00:01:33,061
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where I grew up, born and raised, fourth generation family there.

15
00:01:33,121 --> 00:01:36,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Grew up in this community, care very deeply about this community.

16
00:01:37,206 --> 00:01:40,429
[SPEAKER_01]: I grew up in a working class home, both my parents were custodians.

17
00:01:41,010 --> 00:01:46,635
[SPEAKER_01]: My father passed away when I was young, one out of five children, so I was kind of the adult family member.

18
00:01:47,456 --> 00:01:51,700
[SPEAKER_01]: At a very young age, learned leadership that way from just having to take care of my family.

19
00:01:52,501 --> 00:01:54,683
[SPEAKER_01]: We were fortunate to have my grandmother's house.

20
00:01:54,783 --> 00:02:00,268
[SPEAKER_01]: She bought her house in the 1950s for $12,000, working at the Hostess Cupcake Factory.

21
00:02:00,288 --> 00:02:01,789
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a union job.

22
00:02:02,230 --> 00:02:04,332
[SPEAKER_01]: She took care of her three children on her own.

23
00:02:04,892 --> 00:02:08,675
[SPEAKER_01]: And she was generous enough to let the five of us and my mother live in her home.

24
00:02:09,516 --> 00:02:13,759
[SPEAKER_01]: And I really don't know where my family would be if she didn't let us live in that home.

25
00:02:15,201 --> 00:02:23,668
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't exaggerate to think that my family, we could be dead, we'd be in prison, we'd be doing things that we ended up not doing because we had a home.

26
00:02:24,328 --> 00:02:25,909
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's very important to me.

27
00:02:25,949 --> 00:02:29,190
[SPEAKER_01]: The core tenet of my belief system is having a home for people.

28
00:02:29,810 --> 00:02:34,672
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what my campaign is really about, is affordability, the ability to afford to dream in Massachusetts.

29
00:02:35,392 --> 00:02:42,735
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the number one thing for me, is making sure people have a home, because I know that it kept my family together, and it keeps families together.

30
00:02:42,855 --> 00:02:46,457
[SPEAKER_01]: And I see that as the greatest struggle for working families in Massachusetts.

31
00:02:47,337 --> 00:02:52,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so I grew up here, you know, a lot of people got addicted to drugs in my community.

32
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:54,161
[SPEAKER_01]: They got into crime, violence.

33
00:02:54,401 --> 00:02:59,084
[SPEAKER_01]: I had a very close friend of mine murdered right around the corner from my house, 16 years old.

34
00:02:59,964 --> 00:03:06,308
[SPEAKER_01]: When I was, I joined the army in 2003 to escape a lot of these problems, served two tours in Iraq.

35
00:03:06,688 --> 00:03:11,490
[SPEAKER_01]: And while I was gone, my friend was murdered and I saw this drug epidemic taking over.

36
00:03:11,991 --> 00:03:15,993
[SPEAKER_01]: And I made a commitment to myself while deployed that I was going to come home and serve my community.

37
00:03:16,513 --> 00:03:22,577
[SPEAKER_01]: I came home in 2008, started organizing the community years before I ever even thought about running for office.

38
00:03:22,657 --> 00:03:32,183
[SPEAKER_01]: I was organizing people, having vigils, trying to get local hire for young people in the community, all sorts of things to just represent the neighborhood.

39
00:03:32,543 --> 00:03:39,347
[SPEAKER_01]: And in 2013, I was able to run for office where I won the city council seat board of Alderman at the time.

40
00:03:39,947 --> 00:03:41,108
[SPEAKER_01]: And I've been doing it ever since.

41
00:03:41,708 --> 00:03:43,088
[SPEAKER_01]: I've done a lot in between there.

42
00:03:43,168 --> 00:03:46,350
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, affordability has been my issue for 20 plus years.

43
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:49,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Politicians are talking about it now like it's a new thing.

44
00:03:49,871 --> 00:03:51,012
[SPEAKER_01]: But I've been committed to it.

45
00:03:51,112 --> 00:03:52,152
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a catchphrase now.

46
00:03:52,232 --> 00:03:53,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Everyone's like the affordability candidate.

47
00:03:54,333 --> 00:03:56,474
[SPEAKER_01]: But I've been doing it for 20 plus years.

48
00:03:56,594 --> 00:03:58,675
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen this happening since the 90s.

49
00:03:59,415 --> 00:04:03,377
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's been my commitment to the community to make sure people can afford to live around here.

50
00:04:03,837 --> 00:04:04,817
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a great story.

51
00:04:05,558 --> 00:04:09,099
[SPEAKER_03]: And I got to tell you, there's a lot of similar stories when I grew up, too.

52
00:04:09,239 --> 00:04:12,780
[SPEAKER_03]: I grew up in Method, but my father had a business in Somerville.

53
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:14,540
[SPEAKER_03]: So it's a great story.

54
00:04:15,941 --> 00:04:16,721
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you for that.

55
00:04:17,281 --> 00:04:19,182
[SPEAKER_03]: And also, thank you for your service.

56
00:04:19,322 --> 00:04:22,223
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you both in the military and on the council.

57
00:04:22,723 --> 00:04:26,524
[SPEAKER_03]: I love it when people serve their communities, whatever they're doing.

58
00:04:26,564 --> 00:04:27,725
[SPEAKER_03]: It's it's always nice.

59
00:04:28,465 --> 00:04:29,685
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's a great story.

60
00:04:29,705 --> 00:04:30,045
[SPEAKER_03]: So.

61
00:04:32,792 --> 00:04:37,055
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's get into, since we brought up, you know, affordability and things.

62
00:04:37,255 --> 00:04:56,930
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, with housing and the cost of living continuing to price work and families out of Massachusetts, you know, are there any specific state-level policies or, you know, programs you see that you would champion to lower costs?

63
00:04:56,950 --> 00:04:58,371
[SPEAKER_03]: Is there anything going on now?

64
00:04:58,451 --> 00:05:00,393
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, how do you feel about that?

65
00:05:00,733 --> 00:05:02,094
[SPEAKER_01]: What is there, really?

66
00:05:02,814 --> 00:05:09,518
[SPEAKER_01]: I really believe there's a hundred ways to solve this problem, and one of the issues we have is everyone wants to do things their way.

67
00:05:10,399 --> 00:05:12,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm open to all options.

68
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:16,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, you know, some people want to build housing, other people want to do tenant protections.

69
00:05:17,042 --> 00:05:21,305
[SPEAKER_01]: I think we need both of those things, and we need to be working with each other.

70
00:05:21,745 --> 00:05:26,068
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the obstacle I see right now, is that everybody wants to take their angle.

71
00:05:26,888 --> 00:05:29,471
[SPEAKER_01]: but they won't acknowledge that someone else might have a good idea too.

72
00:05:29,491 --> 00:05:30,752
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'm open to all options.

73
00:05:31,232 --> 00:05:43,964
[SPEAKER_01]: A few things I've done in Somerville, as a city councillor, where I was the chair of the Land Use Committee for many years, I introduced the triple-decker ordinance that people know, which allows the three-family housing

74
00:05:44,304 --> 00:05:45,505
[SPEAKER_01]: throughout the city of Somerville.

75
00:05:45,585 --> 00:05:47,665
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of people are familiar with the triple-decker.

76
00:05:48,086 --> 00:05:50,567
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a form of housing that's unique to New England.

77
00:05:51,127 --> 00:05:52,488
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't find it anywhere else.

78
00:05:53,428 --> 00:05:54,849
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's illegal across the state.

79
00:05:55,169 --> 00:05:59,091
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't build a three-family house anywhere except for Somerville and Cambridge.

80
00:06:00,291 --> 00:06:05,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, because it's not allowed as of right, so you have to get a zone impairment, you have to get a variance.

81
00:06:05,133 --> 00:06:06,013
[SPEAKER_00]: I wasn't aware of that.

82
00:06:06,033 --> 00:06:06,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people don't know.

83
00:06:06,973 --> 00:06:07,654
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they don't know.

84
00:06:07,694 --> 00:06:15,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So a hundred years ago, there was anti-immigrant, anti-working class backlash against the three-family house in particular, the triple-decker.

85
00:06:15,817 --> 00:06:17,597
[SPEAKER_01]: You're old enough to remember the West End.

86
00:06:17,737 --> 00:06:21,019
[SPEAKER_03]: Listen, the West End, as a matter of fact, we were there.

87
00:06:21,079 --> 00:06:22,099
[SPEAKER_03]: We got tossed out of there.

88
00:06:22,339 --> 00:06:22,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

89
00:06:22,779 --> 00:06:24,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So that was all triple deckers.

90
00:06:24,541 --> 00:06:26,082
[SPEAKER_01]: That was a working class community.

91
00:06:26,462 --> 00:06:28,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And people said it was a blighted community.

92
00:06:28,323 --> 00:06:30,205
[SPEAKER_01]: I tore it down to build high rises.

93
00:06:30,945 --> 00:06:34,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So yeah, so 100 years ago, they outlawed it.

94
00:06:34,448 --> 00:06:36,029
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can find this building type anywhere.

95
00:06:36,549 --> 00:06:44,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And I would like to allow that to happen again, allow the three family house which built the working class allows working class families to survive here.

96
00:06:45,055 --> 00:06:46,455
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's just one thing alone.

97
00:06:46,516 --> 00:06:49,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Let the house that exists, exist.

98
00:06:49,636 --> 00:06:53,557
[SPEAKER_01]: And also change the building code to make it cheaper and easier to build that housing.

99
00:06:53,997 --> 00:06:58,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Right now there's a difference in the state building code between building a two-family house and a three-family house.

100
00:06:59,119 --> 00:07:03,560
[SPEAKER_01]: It's significantly more expensive to build a three-family house because it's considered urban residential.

101
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:05,601
[SPEAKER_01]: as opposed to neighborhood residential.

102
00:07:06,021 --> 00:07:12,083
[SPEAKER_01]: If you change that number from two to three, you drop tens of thousands of dollars off the building price, which would reduce the cost.

103
00:07:12,723 --> 00:07:18,605
[SPEAKER_01]: And then one other thing we've done in Somerville, which was affordable housing overlay, which would allow affordable housing to be built.

104
00:07:19,226 --> 00:07:25,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Lots of times in my early activism in Somerville, I saw a lot of affordable housing developments get stopped.

105
00:07:25,468 --> 00:07:33,011
[SPEAKER_01]: because they were short two parking spots, or the building was a floor too tall, and someone would sue and stall the project for years.

106
00:07:33,671 --> 00:07:35,232
[SPEAKER_01]: And that adds to the cost.

107
00:07:35,292 --> 00:07:39,474
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually more expensive to build truly affordable housing than it is market rate housing.

108
00:07:39,934 --> 00:07:41,535
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'd like to make it easier to do that.

109
00:07:41,995 --> 00:07:49,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are just two things we've done, but it really is, there's so many different ways we could attack this, and we just need to get it done.

110
00:07:50,367 --> 00:07:53,228
[SPEAKER_03]: So when I grew up, I mean, you know, we used to go to Revea.

111
00:07:53,668 --> 00:08:00,431
[SPEAKER_03]: A lot of my relatives, when we got thrown out of the West End, went to Beachmont, Medford, Somerville.

112
00:08:01,011 --> 00:08:02,852
[SPEAKER_03]: Somerville had plenty of three-deckers.

113
00:08:03,612 --> 00:08:05,193
[SPEAKER_03]: All my friends are in three-deckers.

114
00:08:05,233 --> 00:08:05,913
[SPEAKER_01]: They're everywhere.

115
00:08:05,993 --> 00:08:09,855
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I mean, I had no idea that they were outlawed.

116
00:08:10,035 --> 00:08:12,896
[SPEAKER_01]: So I wrote a paper about it for school actually.

117
00:08:13,036 --> 00:08:19,718
[SPEAKER_01]: I also should add I graduated from Tufts University on the GI Bill and I just recently got my master's at Harvard.

118
00:08:19,858 --> 00:08:24,580
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm the first in my family in four generations to go to college so I'm very proud of that.

119
00:08:25,020 --> 00:08:35,383
[SPEAKER_01]: But I did a paper on the triple-decker and I learned so the triple-decker house was built by French Canadians, my ancestors, came here they were boat builders and they made the three-family house.

120
00:08:35,903 --> 00:08:39,306
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was made, it was seen as a path forward for the working class.

121
00:08:39,526 --> 00:08:49,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Poor people working in factories could buy this house, they could have either three generations of their family living in that house, or they can rent out two floors to subsidize their own income.

122
00:08:50,355 --> 00:08:52,797
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was a way for people to move out of poverty.

123
00:08:53,237 --> 00:08:59,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it became seen as a blight to people, and they outlawed it, and now you can sell a triple-decker for like a million dollars a floor.

124
00:08:59,943 --> 00:09:01,584
[SPEAKER_01]: So everything's come full circle now.

125
00:09:05,287 --> 00:09:31,107
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean we grew up it was my my grandfather owned the building and it was actually four four stories yeah building beautiful oh really so it was all family I lived in one of the few four-story four-deckers in Somerville huh yeah which is now 100% affordable housing yes because I advocated for that yeah yeah but it was all you know my grandfather lived with us aunts uncles all the cousins whatever it was all fair it was nice it was a nice way to grow up

126
00:09:31,407 --> 00:09:34,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, something worth noting on that, too, because I discovered this recently.

127
00:09:35,031 --> 00:09:37,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So my grandmother just turned 99 years old.

128
00:09:37,253 --> 00:09:37,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Wow.

129
00:09:37,834 --> 00:09:41,758
[SPEAKER_01]: She's the reason we're still in the city at all, if not alive.

130
00:09:41,838 --> 00:09:46,283
[SPEAKER_01]: One of the things I learned, there was a study recently that studied people who make it to 100 years old.

131
00:09:47,384 --> 00:09:53,891
[SPEAKER_01]: I knew England and one of the common themes they found was three family housing because there was always a family member there.

132
00:09:53,911 --> 00:10:02,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So my mother, my grandmother still lives in that house with my mother, my brother and his wife and kid because they can't afford to live anywhere else.

133
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:05,903
[SPEAKER_01]: But they all stick together and they're able to look after my grandmother.

134
00:10:05,983 --> 00:10:07,425
[SPEAKER_01]: She doesn't need home nursing.

135
00:10:07,525 --> 00:10:08,926
[SPEAKER_01]: She's not in a home.

136
00:10:09,046 --> 00:10:10,568
[SPEAKER_01]: She gets to stay where she wants to stay.

137
00:10:10,868 --> 00:10:15,591
[SPEAKER_03]: see now in my opinion, my opinion, which I don't give too often, that's a good situation sometimes.

138
00:10:15,751 --> 00:10:19,153
[SPEAKER_03]: It really is to have family and you take care of each other.

139
00:10:19,733 --> 00:10:20,833
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what I grew up with.

140
00:10:21,134 --> 00:10:26,456
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's what makes us unique and it also is like it's important to have family for everybody.

141
00:10:26,476 --> 00:10:26,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

142
00:10:26,937 --> 00:10:28,618
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's so important to me.

143
00:10:28,658 --> 00:10:29,798
[SPEAKER_03]: A lot of that's missing today.

144
00:10:29,898 --> 00:10:30,799
[SPEAKER_03]: That's all I'll say.

145
00:10:32,432 --> 00:10:32,792
[SPEAKER_03]: All right.

146
00:10:32,873 --> 00:10:34,835
[SPEAKER_03]: So, good answer.

147
00:10:34,895 --> 00:10:35,856
[SPEAKER_03]: I appreciate that.

148
00:10:35,936 --> 00:10:38,999
[SPEAKER_03]: And I, for one, learned something.

149
00:10:39,099 --> 00:10:41,341
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm sure a lot of people learned something from that answer.

150
00:10:42,742 --> 00:10:52,652
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you know, what is your position on the proposed, you know, TARPA legislation, you know, the Tenant Opportunity to Purchase Act?

151
00:10:52,732 --> 00:10:54,254
[SPEAKER_03]: I know, is it still in the bill?

152
00:10:54,354 --> 00:10:54,714
[SPEAKER_03]: It's out?

153
00:10:55,015 --> 00:10:55,335
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

154
00:10:55,435 --> 00:11:07,963
[SPEAKER_01]: So I heard today that it's the tenant opportunity to purchase, which is also known as right of first refusal, which would basically allow, if a building is being sold, that the tenant living there would have the first option to buy.

155
00:11:08,823 --> 00:11:14,627
[SPEAKER_01]: So I support that because it's one of the many hundred things we could do to help people stay where they want to stay.

156
00:11:15,427 --> 00:11:18,288
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not surprised in the least that it was taken out of the bill.

157
00:11:18,708 --> 00:11:25,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the city of Somerville, we introduced tenant right of first refusal probably a decade ago by a homeowner petition.

158
00:11:25,270 --> 00:11:27,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Went to the state house, nothing's happened with it.

159
00:11:28,190 --> 00:11:34,432
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I saw that it was being reintroduced this year and it was being used as like, hey, we're gonna do this, I was like, that's not happening.

160
00:11:34,572 --> 00:11:38,113
[SPEAKER_01]: I just know it's not gonna happen because we've been down this road so many times.

161
00:11:38,713 --> 00:11:44,175
[SPEAKER_01]: And what frustrates me, what I said earlier about everyone wants to focus on their one thing.

162
00:11:44,995 --> 00:11:48,499
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, that is one good thing that has repeatedly failed.

163
00:11:49,319 --> 00:11:50,701
[SPEAKER_01]: And what more are we going to do?

164
00:11:50,761 --> 00:11:51,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Can we do something else?

165
00:11:51,982 --> 00:11:55,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Can we adapt and figure out like, what can we get out of the same legislation?

166
00:11:56,426 --> 00:12:03,633
[SPEAKER_01]: I've really come to the conclusion when it comes to affordable housing, that sometimes like people submit these things knowing that they'll fail.

167
00:12:04,234 --> 00:12:06,375
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they throw their hands up and say, what can you do?

168
00:12:06,455 --> 00:12:09,075
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the state legislature just won't get out of the way.

169
00:12:09,095 --> 00:12:15,097
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, but there's a lot of things we can do, like the accessory dwelling units, that passed at the state level.

170
00:12:15,297 --> 00:12:19,058
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's something that they were able to get through because there was a consensus around it.

171
00:12:19,498 --> 00:12:28,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So I support the right to purchase, tenant opportunity to purchase, because even myself, like when a house has been sold,

172
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:31,142
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be nice for the tenant to be able to stay there.

173
00:12:31,162 --> 00:12:31,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

174
00:12:31,683 --> 00:12:35,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Most tenants are not going to be able to afford the house that they're renting out of.

175
00:12:35,486 --> 00:12:35,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

176
00:12:35,926 --> 00:12:37,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise they'd already be owning their home.

177
00:12:37,888 --> 00:12:38,649
[SPEAKER_02]: That's correct.

178
00:12:38,709 --> 00:12:44,094
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's like, so I felt like I saw it coming and I was like, heard people talking about it.

179
00:12:44,114 --> 00:12:46,436
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm like, this isn't happening, but what can happen?

180
00:12:46,476 --> 00:12:46,676
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

181
