[SPEAKER_01]: So I support Diana DiZaglio's the state auditor and her audit attempts.

368
00:25:06,296 --> 00:25:12,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Both of my opponents at the statehouse have opposed the audit through their own votes, and I would have supported it.

369
00:25:12,400 --> 00:25:14,241
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's one difference between me and them.

370
00:25:14,261 --> 00:25:22,646
[SPEAKER_01]: I think a lot of it, when it comes down to transparency, I just think about my role as a city councilor and how we respect open meeting law.

371
00:25:23,166 --> 00:25:30,671
[SPEAKER_01]: So in Somerville and in every city across Massachusetts, we are held to open meeting law standards, which means all of our votes are recorded.

372
00:25:31,311 --> 00:25:32,972
[SPEAKER_01]: All of our meetings happen in public.

373
00:25:33,392 --> 00:25:36,014
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a minute, they're provided minutes of those meetings.

374
00:25:36,654 --> 00:25:42,778
[SPEAKER_01]: As the chair, as the president of the city council for three years, I made sure all of our meetings were video recorded.

375
00:25:43,459 --> 00:25:45,080
[SPEAKER_01]: We transcribed them into languages.

376
00:25:45,420 --> 00:25:47,161
[SPEAKER_01]: We transcribed them into braille.

377
00:25:48,202 --> 00:25:56,127
[SPEAKER_01]: And there were times when I actually filed an open meeting law complaint against my own colleagues because I felt like they were doing things behind closed doors.

378
00:25:56,608 --> 00:26:09,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So there are times when you're allowed to, it's called executive session, where you're legally allowed to have a discussion behind closed doors, but you have to have it in a certain way and you can't talk about certain things.

379
00:26:10,177 --> 00:26:32,308
[SPEAKER_01]: that wasn't happening so I stood up for that and those people later thanked me like they actually were like oh you were right we didn't realize that we were going against the rules here and so every city has open meeting law but the state has exempted themselves from it so they don't record the minutes they don't record the votes uh deals can happen behind closed doors and the worst thing my experience is

380
00:26:33,368 --> 00:26:59,125
[SPEAKER_01]: uh... having things uh... sent to study that's what happens at the state house is i go up to the state house i testify in support of meaningful legislation i have state representative smile tell me what a great job i did thank you for your service and then it gets sent to study which basically means the bill is dead with no vote it means it's over it's over and there's no explanation about this whatsoever and it's like as a counselor

381
00:26:59,645 --> 00:27:02,829
[SPEAKER_01]: There's lots of times when there's something before us that isn't ready.

382
00:27:03,069 --> 00:27:05,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Like we're not ready for a vote.

383
00:27:05,612 --> 00:27:08,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So it gets tabled, which is kind of the same as being sent to study.

384
00:27:08,735 --> 00:27:11,198
[SPEAKER_01]: But you at least get an explanation as to why.

385
00:27:11,498 --> 00:27:15,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Your elected officials have to stand up and say, this is why I'm not ready to vote for this.

386
00:27:16,243 --> 00:27:17,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why we're tabling it.

387
00:27:17,765 --> 00:27:38,069
[SPEAKER_01]: there it just gets sent to study there's no discussion about it whatsoever and i i've said often that you know i don't think people would care as much about transparency if things were getting done if they were meeting behind closed doors to make amazing deals for the state then nobody would care no but it's a fact that things are going there to die and no one knows why

388
00:27:38,629 --> 00:27:48,192
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why I would like to make sure open meeting law applies to the state legislature, just like the city, just like the majority of states in this country.

389
00:27:48,312 --> 00:27:51,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Florida has open meeting law, so they're more transparent than we are.

390
00:27:52,154 --> 00:27:57,816
[SPEAKER_01]: And I do think that's a huge reason why things aren't getting done, because you literally can't hold your elected officials accountable.

391
00:27:57,836 --> 00:28:01,497
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to take their word for it that they're doing the right thing, because there's no record of it.

392
00:28:01,897 --> 00:28:02,157
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

393
00:28:02,257 --> 00:28:02,538
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

394
00:28:02,918 --> 00:28:03,859
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, that's incredible.

395
00:28:04,720 --> 00:28:08,844
[SPEAKER_03]: I it's good to hear someone say that.

